  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f({a}_{1})\\ne 0$ and $g({a}_{2})\\ne 0,$ where ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are distinct points in $[0,3],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({a}_{1})+g({a}_{1}) & \\ne 0 \\\\ f({a}_{2})+g({a}_{2}) & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)+g(x)\\ne 0$ at two distinct points. So, $N$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(a)\\ne 0$ for some $a\\in [0,3],$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\n$$\n\n \nfor any $x\\in [0,3].$ So, $N$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $N$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
